@meta
id: 20-01
title: The paste that came out wrong
chapter: 20
type: lesson
skills: register-zero
par: 35
minutes: 5
vscode: copy, then cut something else, then paste — and get the cut
open: order.provider.spec.ts

@brief
You have been using `y`, `d` and `p` since chapter 8 without being told
where the text goes. It goes into a register: a named slot vim keeps
text in. The one `p` reads from is called the unnamed register, and
here is the catch that has bitten every vim user at least once: both
yank *and* delete write to it. Copy a line, delete something on the way
to where you wanted it, press `p`, and you get the deleted thing.

The second test in this spec never mocks the repository. It needs the
same `reveal(orderRepository)...` line the first test has, directly
under `order.status = 'SHIPPED';`. The first test also has a leftover
`console.log(order);` that has to go.

Do it in this order: copy the reveal line, delete the console line,
then go to the second test and put. Watch what comes out. Then fix it
with the register that only yanks write to.

@keys
| `""` | the unnamed register — what plain `p` reads from |
| `"0` | the yank register — the last thing you *yanked*, never a delete |
| `"0p` | put from the yank register |
| `:reg` | show every register and what is in it |

@hints
Do the yank, then the delete, then plain `p`. It puts the console line.
`u` to take it back. The reveal line is still in vim; it is just not in
the register `p` reads.
--
Every put can name its register with `"` and one character in front of
it. `"0` is the register that only yanks write to.
--
`/reveal(` Enter, `yy`, `j`, `dd`, `/status =` Enter, `"0p`. (Plain
`/reveal` finds the import first; the `(` skips it.)

@solution
    /reveal(<Enter> yy j dd /status =<Enter> "0p :wq<Enter>

Vim keeps the last yank separately in `"0`, so yank-then-delete-then-put
has always had a clean answer. Most people learn it after the third time
they paste the wrong thing. Now you know it before the first.

The rule to carry: `p` gives you the most recent *anything*; `"0p` gives
you the most recent *yank*. When you have yanked something you care
about and there is any delete between you and the paste, reach for
`"0p` without thinking about it.

There is a second reason `"0` matters on this machine. Your vimrc has
`clipboard=unnamedplus`, which routes the unnamed register through the
system clipboard. That is convenient, and it means anything you copy in
a browser lands in `""` too. `"0` is untouched by any of that. Lesson
20-13 goes into the clipboard properly.

@start order.provider.spec.ts
import { stub, reveal } from 'jest-auto-stub';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { buildOrder } from '../../../test/factories/order.factory';

describe('OrderProvider', () => {
  const orderRepository = stub<OrderRepository>();
  const paymentService = stub<PaymentService>();
  const provider = new OrderProvider(orderRepository, paymentService);
  const order = buildOrder({ status: 'PENDING' });

  it('cancels a pending order', async () => {
    reveal(orderRepository).findOne.mockResolvedValue(order);
    console.log(order);
    await provider.cancel(order.id);
    expect(reveal(paymentService).refund).toHaveBeenCalledWith(order.id);
  });

  it('refuses to cancel a shipped order', async () => {
    order.status = 'SHIPPED';
    await expect(provider.cancel(order.id)).rejects.toThrow('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
  });
});

@expected order.provider.spec.ts
import { stub, reveal } from 'jest-auto-stub';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { buildOrder } from '../../../test/factories/order.factory';

describe('OrderProvider', () => {
  const orderRepository = stub<OrderRepository>();
  const paymentService = stub<PaymentService>();
  const provider = new OrderProvider(orderRepository, paymentService);
  const order = buildOrder({ status: 'PENDING' });

  it('cancels a pending order', async () => {
    reveal(orderRepository).findOne.mockResolvedValue(order);
    await provider.cancel(order.id);
    expect(reveal(paymentService).refund).toHaveBeenCalledWith(order.id);
  });

  it('refuses to cancel a shipped order', async () => {
    order.status = 'SHIPPED';
    reveal(orderRepository).findOne.mockResolvedValue(order);
    await expect(provider.cancel(order.id)).rejects.toThrow('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
  });
});
